WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:18.000
Compare a thin sheet, eight fine axes and one thick axis. Each contains one cubic centimetre of tissue and, at the assumed common fresh density, one gram fresh mass. Predict the surface areas.

00:18.000 --> 00:24.000
Count both faces and edges of the sheet, and the sides and ends of every cylinder. Surface areas are 21.30, 20.50 and 5.57 square centimetres.

00:24.000 --> 00:36.000
Unfold the surfaces at a common area scale. Green shows broad faces or unrolled cylinder walls; gold shows edges or circular ends. Gaps separate patches for counting. The dimensions determine surface area to volume ratio.

00:36.000 --> 00:56.000
At the same surface concentration and uptake activity per unit surface, whole-thallus uptake equals surface area times areal flux. Rates are 1.420, 1.367 and 0.371 micromoles N per hour.

00:56.000 --> 01:14.000
The greatest distance from the tissue interior to a surface is 0.5, 1.0 and 5.0 millimetres in these geometries. These are internal distances, not external boundary-layer thicknesses.

01:14.000 --> 01:26.000
For similar shapes, doubling every linear dimension multiplies area by four and volume by eight. Surface area to volume ratio halves. This size comparison does not hold tissue volume constant.

01:26.000 --> 01:40.000
Keep the same eight axes and total surface. Prescribe different water renewal rates, with the same incoming nitrate concentration. Predict the concentration within the canopy water and the uptake rate.

01:40.000 --> 01:53.000
The well-renewed model has canopy concentration 8.70 micromoles per litre and uptake 1.302 micromoles per hour. The sheltered model has concentration 0.78 and uptake 0.277.

01:53.000 --> 02:06.000
At steady state, incoming N minus outgoing N equals uptake. Surface area and areal uptake parameters are unchanged. Exchange is prescribed; the animation does not calculate flow from the spacing of the axes.

02:06.000 --> 02:26.000
Now increase area at fixed water renewal. Uptake approaches but cannot exceed the incoming supply of 0.300 micromoles per hour. These are steady comparisons, not a time course of growth.

02:26.000 --> 02:46.000
Hyaline hairs can affect exchange surface and the surrounding water. Hair production alone does not establish a lower intrinsic half-saturation constant. Compare measurements that distinguish the proposed mechanisms.

02:46.000 --> 03:02.000
Compare uptake per unit surface at matched local nutrient concentration. Change water renewal with tissue and area held fixed. Match light, temperature and nutritional history, and state contrasting predictions.

03:02.000 --> 03:10.000
Next, follow acquired nitrogen into internal pools and new tissue. Can uptake continue without immediate growth, and can growth continue using stored nitrogen?
